      "category": "history",
      "quality": "good"
    },
    {
      "id": 2,
      "text": "The town’s name comes from the Spanish word for duck, reflecting the many ducks that early visitor saw near the river confluence.",
      "sourceSentenceIds": [
        1,
        3
      ],
      "sourceLabels": [
        "Wikipedia"
      ],
      "source": "https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Pateros%2C_Washington",
      "category": "etymology",
      "quality": "good"
    },
    {
      "id": 3,
      "text": "Wells Dam, completed in 1967, flooded most of the original city and created a 20‑mile reservoir now called Lake Pateros.",
      "sourceSentenceIds": [
        5,
        28,
        29
      ],
      "sourceLabels": [
        "Web source 1",
        "Wikipedia"
      ],
      "source": "https://www.pateros.com/recreation/pateros_museum.php",
      "category": "history",
      "quality": "good"
    },
    {
      "id": 4,
      "text": "In 1914 the Great Northern Railroad finished, turning Pateros into a main rail hub between Wenatchee and Oroville.",
